\section{METHODOLOGY}
\label{sec:methodology}

We present a federated knowledge distillation framework where client teacher outputs are kept encrypted during transfer and server-side processing. Figure~\ref{fig:overview} shows the protocol. The method has three phases: (1) client-side local training, (2) encrypted feature extraction and upload, and (3) server-side encrypted distillation. We use two student modes: a reduced HE-compatible model (\textit{HE-Deep}, 5-layer CNN) with logit supervision, and a full model (\textit{PolyResNet-18}, 11.17M parameters) with progressive stacking.

\subsection{System Model and Threat Model}
\label{sec:system_model}

\subsubsection{Participants and Communication Model}
We consider $N$ clients, each with a private dataset $\mathcal{D}_i$, and a central server with access to a public auxiliary dataset $\mathcal{X}_{\text{aux}}$ but no private client data. Communication has one required training phase and one optional release phase:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Training upload phase:} Each client encrypts teacher outputs under CKKS and sends ciphertexts to the server.
    \item \textbf{Optional release phase:} If release policy requires threshold control, clients send threshold shares only at model release or final audit.
\end{enumerate}

No additional client interaction is required during the training loop.

\subsubsection{Threat Model}
We assume a \textit{semi-honest} (honest-but-curious) server: the server follows the protocol but may attempt to infer information about client data. Clients are assumed honest. During training, the server does not receive individual plaintext teacher outputs $f_i$.

This guarantee is structural: client targets remain encrypted while the server performs training operations. For stronger formal guarantees, calibrated differential privacy noise can be added before encryption.

\subsubsection{Encryption Parameters}
Since the server performs only homomorphic \textit{addition} on ciphertexts (no multiplication), the CKKS parameters can be minimal: $\log N = 13$ (polynomial degree 8192), two coefficient modulus primes, providing 128-bit security at zero multiplicative depth. This yields ciphertexts of approximately 256~KB each, smaller than the ${\sim}$1.2~MB ciphertexts that would be required for encrypted inference ($\log N = 14$, 18 primes for 17 multiplicative levels).

\subsection{Phase 1: Client-Side Local Training}
\label{sec:phase1}

Each client $i$ trains a local ResNet-18 teacher $T_i$ on their private partition $\mathcal{D}_i$ using standard supervised learning with ReLU activations and batch normalization. This phase is fully local and private---no data leaves the client device.

\textbf{Shared initialization.} To enable multi-client feature averaging in Phase~3, the server distributes a common random initialization $\theta_0$ to all clients before local training. Each client fine-tunes from $\theta_0$ on their private data. This is standard practice in federated learning and does not constitute a privacy violation: $\theta_0$ is randomly generated and contains no private information.

Data is partitioned across clients using a Dirichlet distribution with concentration parameter $\alpha$, where $\alpha \to 0$ produces extreme non-IID partitions (clients see few classes) and $\alpha \to \infty$ produces IID partitions.

\subsection{Phase 2: Feature Extraction and CKKS Encryption}
\label{sec:phase2}

Each client runs their trained teacher on auxiliary data $\mathcal{X}_{\text{aux}}$ or on a local input batch and extracts:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Mode A (logit-only):} Final logits $z_i(x) \in \mathbb{R}^C$ for each auxiliary sample $x$.
    \item \textbf{Mode B (full features):} Intermediate features at each block boundary---stem, layer1--4 outputs, and logits---yielding 188,426 scalars per sample for ResNet-18 on CIFAR-10.
\end{itemize}

All extracted features are post-BatchNorm, post-ReLU outputs from the teacher network. Clients encrypt these values under CKKS before uploading to the server.

\subsubsection{Feature Alignment via Shared Initialization}
\label{sec:alignment}

Multi-client feature averaging in Mode~B requires that teachers produce \textit{channel-aligned} intermediate representations: channel $j$ in teacher $T_1$ must encode similar concepts to channel $j$ in teacher $T_2$, so that element-wise averaging produces a meaningful consensus target.

Logit-level aggregation (Mode~A) does not face this challenge, since logit dimensions correspond to fixed class indices and are inherently aligned across all teachers.

For intermediate features, shared initialization $\theta_0$ provides the alignment mechanism. Because all teachers start from the same random point and are trained with the same architecture, early and middle layer representations remain well-aligned even after independent fine-tuning on heterogeneous data. We validate this empirically via pairwise linear Centered Kernel Alignment (CKA) \cite{kornblith2019similarity} between teacher feature representations at each layer, under three conditions of varying difficulty:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Easy:} $N{=}4, \alpha{=}0.5$ (moderate heterogeneity)
    \item \textbf{Hard 1:} $N{=}4, \alpha{=}0.01$ (extreme non-IID)
    \item \textbf{Hard 2:} $N{=}8, \alpha{=}0.1$ (more clients, moderate non-IID)
\end{enumerate}

Our pre-registered decision thresholds are: if per-layer CKA~$> 0.7$ for all layers across all conditions, we proceed with full feature averaging; if CKA drops below 0.5 at deeper layers, we restrict Mode~B to early-block features with logit aggregation for deeper blocks.

\subsubsection{Communication Cost Analysis}
\label{sec:communication}

With CKKS parameters $\log N = 13$ and 4096 slots per ciphertext, Mode~B requires $\lceil 188{,}426 / 4096 \rceil = 46$ ciphertexts per sample (${\sim}$11.5~MB), while Mode~A packs approximately 409 samples per ciphertext, requiring only 13 ciphertexts for 5000 auxiliary samples (${\sim}$3.3~MB total).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Per-client upload cost at various auxiliary data ratios}
\label{tab:communication}
\begin{tabular}{r|r|r|r}
\hline
\textbf{Aux \%} & \textbf{Samples} & \textbf{Mode B} & \textbf{Mode A} \\
\hline
1\% & 500 & 5.7 GB & 0.5 MB \\
5\% & 2,500 & 28.7 GB & 2.0 MB \\
10\% & 5,000 & 57.5 GB & 3.3 MB \\
20\% & 10,000 & 115 GB & 6.4 MB \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Mode~B communication is substantial but one-shot. At 10\% auxiliary data, Mode~B requires about 57.5~GB upload per client, while Mode~A requires about 3.3~MB. Release-stage threshold traffic is optional and reported separately in Section~\ref{sec:experiments}. With magnitude-regularised training (Section~\ref{para:magreg}), Mode~B is validated up to $N{=}32$ clients (Section~\ref{sec:experiments}).

\subsection{Phase 3: Server-Side Encrypted Distillation}
\label{sec:phase3}

After receiving encrypted uploads, the server performs the required encrypted aggregation and supervision steps directly on encrypted targets. Threshold decryption is not part of the training loop.

\subsubsection{Mode B---Full Model: PolyResNet-18 with Progressive Stacking}
\label{sec:mode_b}

The server trains a full PolyResNet-18 student block-by-block against averaged teacher features:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item For each block $k \in \{0, \ldots, 5\}$ (stem, layer1--4, FC), the server holds $N$ encrypted feature sets $\llbracket f_i^{(k)} \rrbracket$.
    \item \textbf{Homomorphic aggregation:} $\llbracket \bar{f}^{(k)} \rrbracket = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i} \llbracket f_i^{(k)} \rrbracket$ (addition only, zero multiplicative depth).
    \item \textbf{Student-input progressive stacking:} Blocks are trained sequentially; each block receives the frozen student output from the preceding block, matching inference data flow.
    \item \textbf{Magnitude-regularised loss:} A log-ratio magnitude penalty prevents scale drift that causes NaN divergence with polynomial activations (Section~\ref{para:magnitude}).
    \item \textbf{End-to-end server-side training:} Aggregation and block-by-block training complete on the server without further client interaction.
\end{enumerate}

The progressive stacking approach is useful for polynomial activation networks: standard end-to-end knowledge distillation with polynomial activations often diverges to NaN or yields near-random accuracy (Section~\ref{sec:experiments}).

\subsubsection{Mode A---Reduced Model: HE-Deep with Logit Distillation}
\label{sec:mode_a}

For communication-constrained settings, Mode~A provides a lightweight alternative. The server averages the encrypted logits from $N$ clients via homomorphic addition:
\begin{equation}
    \llbracket \bar{z}(x) \rrbracket = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^{N} \llbracket z_i(x) \rrbracket
\end{equation}
The server trains \textit{HE-Deep}---a 5-layer polynomial CNN---using mean squared error (MSE) on logits:
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{L}_{\text{MSE}} = \frac{1}{C} \sum_{c=1}^{C} \left( s_c(x) - \bar{z}_c(x) \right)^2
\end{equation}
where $s(x)$ are student logits and $\bar{z}(x)$ are aggregated teacher logits. We use MSE rather than KL divergence because softmax is not a polynomial operation and is not suitable for encrypted-logit processing.

\paragraph{Magnitude Accumulation Problem.}
\label{para:magnitude}
Na\"ive progressive stacking feeds each block the \emph{student's own} composed output from all preceding frozen blocks. While conceptually natural (each block trains on the representation it will see at inference), this creates an instability with polynomial activations: the quadratic term in $\text{PolyAct}(x) = 0.3x^2 + 0.7x$ amplifies activation magnitudes at every block. By layer3, five polynomial activations have been composed (stem $+$ 2$\times$layer1 $+$ 2$\times$layer2), inflating magnitudes from ${\sim}\!\pm1$ to ${\sim}\!\pm5$--$10$. The next polynomial then squares these values ($0.3 \times 50 \approx 15$), causing NaN divergence.

The normalized MSE loss (Eq.~\ref{eq:normmse}) is scale-\emph{invariant}: the student can perfectly match the teacher's spatial structure while growing to $10\times$ the magnitude, incurring no training penalty. This is acceptable for the \emph{current} block's loss but problematic when the output is fed through subsequent frozen blocks that further amplify the scale. For $N{=}1$ and $N{=}2$, averaged teacher features are sufficiently well-conditioned that ChannelScale normalization controls magnitudes. For $N{\geq}4$, increased feature diversity from more clients destabilises this equilibrium, and ${\sim}$90\% of runs diverge at layer3.

\paragraph{Magnitude-Regularised Progressive Stacking.}
\label{para:magreg}
We address the magnitude accumulation problem while preserving train--inference consistency: each block receives \emph{frozen student output} from the preceding block (matching the exact data flow at inference), but is trained with a magnitude-regularised loss that prevents unbounded scale drift.

The loss combines NormMSE for structural matching with a log-ratio penalty that constrains student feature magnitudes to match teacher targets:
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{L}_{\text{MagReg}} = \mathcal{L}_{\text{NormMSE}}(\hat{f}, \bar{f}) + \lambda \cdot \frac{1}{C} \sum_{c=1}^{C} \left(\log \frac{\sigma_{\hat{f}_c}}{\sigma_{\bar{f}_c}}\right)^{\!2}
    \label{eq:magreg}
\end{equation}
where $\sigma_{\hat{f}_c}$ and $\sigma_{\bar{f}_c}$ are the per-channel standard deviations of student and teacher features over the batch and spatial dimensions, and $\lambda = 1.0$. The log-ratio form is symmetric and scale-free: deviations in either direction (student too large or too small) are penalised equally, and the penalty is proportional to the \emph{multiplicative} mismatch rather than the absolute one.

This directly addresses the training instability mechanism: NormMSE alone is scale-invariant and permits unbounded magnitude growth; the log-ratio penalty addresses this issue by penalising any discrepancy between student and teacher scales. Student-input training is preserved, reducing the train--inference distribution gap that causes alternative approaches (teacher-input training with post-hoc affine bridges) to collapse to random accuracy.

\paragraph{Collaborative Feature Normalisation.}
\label{para:collab_norm}
Clients compute per-channel statistics to keep encrypted targets numerically bounded before upload. In practice, this can be implemented as mean/variance-based affine rescaling on the client side or as secure moment sharing among clients. The training loss in Eq.~\ref{eq:magreg} then controls remaining student--teacher magnitude mismatch during blockwise training.

\textbf{Normalized MSE loss.} Each block is trained with per-channel normalized MSE:
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{L}_{\text{NormMSE}} = \frac{1}{C} \sum_{c=1}^{C} \text{MSE}\!\left(\frac{\hat{f}_c - \mu_{\hat{f}_c}}{\sigma_{\hat{f}_c}}, \frac{\bar{f}_c - \mu_{\bar{f}_c}}{\sigma_{\bar{f}_c}}\right)
    \label{eq:normmse}
\end{equation}
where $\hat{f}_c$ and $\bar{f}_c$ are the student and averaged teacher features for channel $c$, normalized to zero mean and unit variance over the batch and spatial dimensions. This scale-invariant loss is useful: without normalization, accuracy drops from ${\sim}$91\% to ${\sim}$55\% due to magnitude mismatches between polynomial and ReLU activation ranges.

\subsection{HE-Compatible Architecture Design}
\label{sec:architecture}

Both student architectures use only HE-compatible operations---no ReLU, batch normalization, or other operations that require comparison or division under encryption. We detail each component's CKKS implementation below.

\subsubsection{Operation-Level CKKS Decomposition}
\label{sec:he_ops}

We enumerate every distinct operation in PolyResNet-18 and its CKKS cost:

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{CKKS primitive costs for each PolyResNet-18 operation. ``PT'' = plaintext (known weights), ``CT'' = ciphertext (encrypted activations).}
\label{tab:he_ops}
\begin{tabular}{l|l|c}
\hline
\textbf{Operation} & \textbf{CKKS Primitive} & \textbf{Mult.\ Levels} \\
\hline
Convolution (all layers) & PT$\times$CT + CT addition & 0 \\
ChannelScale ($\gamma x + \beta$) & PT$\times$CT + PT addition & 0 \\
PolyAct ($ax^2 + bx + c$) & CT$\times$CT + PT$\times$CT + add & 1 \\
Residual addition ($x + F(x)$) & CT + CT & 0 \\
Average pooling & Sum(CT) $\times$ PT scalar & 0 \\
FC layer ($Wx + b$) & PT$\times$CT + PT addition & 0 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\textbf{Convolution} is a linear operation: each output element is a weighted sum of input elements with fixed (plaintext) kernel weights. Under CKKS this decomposes into plaintext-ciphertext multiplications and ciphertext additions, consuming zero multiplicative levels.

\textbf{ChannelScale} ($\gamma \odot x + \beta$) replaces batch normalization.
Batch normalization requires computing running means ($\mu$) and variances ($\sigma^2$)---operations that involve division ($x / \sigma$) and square root ($\sqrt{\sigma^2}$). Under CKKS, division requires iterative Goldschmidt approximation consuming ${\sim}$10 multiplicative levels per invocation, making even a single BN layer prohibitively expensive. ChannelScale provides equivalent per-channel affine capacity:
\begin{equation}
    \text{ChannelScale}(x) = \gamma \odot x + \beta
\end{equation}
where $\gamma, \beta \in \mathbb{R}^C$ are per-channel learnable parameters. Since both $\gamma$ and $\beta$ are known plaintext values at inference, this is a plaintext-ciphertext multiplication plus plaintext-ciphertext addition, consuming \textit{zero} multiplicative levels. Without ChannelScale, polynomial networks diverge to NaN at layer3 due to uncontrolled activation magnitudes across channels.

\textbf{Polynomial Activation (PolyAct)} replaces ReLU:
\begin{equation}
    \text{PolyAct}(x) = ax^2 + bx + c
\end{equation}
with learnable parameters initialized at $a = 0.3$, $b = 0.7$, $c = 0$. ReLU is a comparison operation ($\max(0, x)$) that has no efficient polynomial representation under CKKS. PolyAct computes $x^2$ via one ciphertext-ciphertext multiplication (1 level), then forms the degree-2 polynomial via plaintext-scalar multiplications and additions (0 levels). Total: \textbf{1 multiplicative level} per activation. After training, $a$, $b$, $c$ are frozen and become plaintext constants.

\textbf{Residual addition} ($x + F(x)$) is a ciphertext-ciphertext addition, consuming zero levels.

\textbf{Average pooling} ($\text{AvgPool}(x) = \frac{1}{HW}\sum_{h,w} x_{h,w}$) is a sum of ciphertext elements followed by multiplication with the plaintext scalar $\frac{1}{HW}$, consuming zero multiplicative levels. Under slot-packed CKKS, spatial pooling is implemented via ciphertext rotations and additions, which are relatively expensive computationally but do not consume multiplicative levels.

\textbf{FC layer} ($Wx + b$) is plaintext-ciphertext matrix-vector multiplication plus plaintext bias, consuming zero levels.

\subsubsection{PolyBasicBlock: Composite CKKS Analysis}
\label{sec:polyblock}

Each PolyBasicBlockV2 follows the dataflow:
\begin{equation}
    x \xrightarrow{\text{conv}_1} \xrightarrow{\text{CS}_1} \xrightarrow{\text{PolyAct}_1} \xrightarrow{\text{conv}_2} \xrightarrow{\text{CS}_2} \xrightarrow{+\,\text{identity}} \xrightarrow{\text{PolyAct}_2} y
\end{equation}
where $\text{conv}_1, \text{conv}_2, \text{CS}_1, \text{CS}_2$, residual addition, and the optional downsample (conv $+$ ChannelScale) all consume zero multiplicative levels. Only the two PolyAct invocations consume levels:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\text{PolyAct}_1$: current depth $d \to d+1$
    \item $\text{PolyAct}_2$: current depth $d+1 \to d+2$
\end{itemize}
Thus each block consumes \textbf{2 multiplicative levels}. The downsample path (present in stride-2 blocks) consists of a $1{\times}1$ convolution and ChannelScale---both zero-level operations---so the residual addition does not introduce depth asymmetry.

\subsubsection{CKKS Inference-Level Budget}
Table~\ref{tab:ckks_levels} summarizes the sequential multiplicative depth for the complete PolyResNet-18 forward pass.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{CKKS multiplicative levels for PolyResNet-18 encrypted inference}
\label{tab:ckks_levels}
\begin{tabular}{l|c|c|c}
\hline
\textbf{Component} & \textbf{Levels/Block} & \textbf{Blocks} & \textbf{Levels Total} \\
\hline
Stem (conv $+$ CS $+$ PolyAct) & --- & 1 & 1 \\
Layer 1 & 2 & 2 & 4 \\
Layer 2 & 2 & 2 & 4 \\
Layer 3 & 2 & 2 & 4 \\
Layer 4 & 2 & 2 & 4 \\
Average pooling & --- & --- & 0 \\
FC (linear) & --- & --- & 0 \\
\hline
\textbf{Total} & & & \textbf{17} \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

With 17 multiplicative levels, the required CKKS parameters for encrypted inference are: polynomial degree $\log N = 14$ (ring dimension 16384), 18 coefficient modulus primes (17 levels $+$ 1 for the encryption scale), each of 40--50 bits, providing 128-bit security. This yields ciphertexts of approximately 1.2~MB each. A lighter client-side model (HE-Deep, 5 layers, $\leq$5 levels) would permit $\log N = 13$ with $\sim$256~KB ciphertexts.

These 17 levels are relevant only for \textit{encrypted inference} after deployment. During training (Phases~1--3), the encrypted path is dominated by low-depth aggregation and HE-compatible supervision operations, so the parameter budget remains lower than deployment-time encrypted inference.

\subsubsection{Encrypted Training Path and Implementation Scope}
\label{sec:encrypted_training}

The training design is defined for encrypted target processing: clients upload bounded encrypted targets, and the server applies blockwise supervision using HE-compatible operations. Our current implementation combines direct CKKS kernels and protocol-aligned simulation components to keep experiments tractable at scale. This setup is used to measure utility and stability while preserving the protocol structure: encrypted uploads, blockwise supervision, and polynomial student operations.

\subsection{Training Stability}
\label{sec:stability}

Polynomial activation networks present unique training challenges that require multiple interlocking stability mechanisms:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Magnitude-regularised loss} (Eq.~\ref{eq:magreg}) combines NormMSE for structural matching with a log-ratio penalty that prevents unbounded scale growth through frozen polynomial blocks.
    \item \textbf{NormMSE loss} (Eq.~\ref{eq:normmse}) provides scale invariance needed for matching polynomial-range student features to ReLU-range teacher features.
    \item \textbf{ChannelScale} prevents magnitude explosion by providing per-channel normalization capacity without the HE cost of batch normalization.
    \item \textbf{Gradient clipping} ($\text{max\_norm} = 1.0$) prevents gradient explosion from the quadratic term in PolyAct.
    \item \textbf{Student-input progressive stacking} preserves train--inference consistency so that each block trains on the same distribution it receives at test time.
\end{enumerate}

We conduct a systematic ablation study (Section~\ref{sec:ablation}) to isolate the contribution of each component. The ablation shows that the magnitude-regularised loss is \emph{necessary in our settings}: pure NormMSE (scale-invariant, no magnitude penalty) allows unbounded growth through frozen polynomial blocks, causing NaN divergence for $N{\geq}4$ clients. An alternative approach---training blocks on teacher features rather than student features to avoid magnitude accumulation, then inserting post-hoc affine bridges at inference---collapses to random accuracy (10\%) because per-channel affine transforms cannot capture the complex distributional differences between polynomial and ReLU feature spaces.

\subsection{Protocol Security Analysis}
\label{sec:security}

\textbf{What is protected:} Individual teacher outputs $f_i$ are encrypted under CKKS during upload and aggregation. The server never observes any individual client's teacher features or logits in plaintext.

\textbf{What may be revealed under policy:} If threshold release is enabled, only the $N$-client averaged consensus $\bar{f}$ is recoverable; individual client outputs remain hidden.

\textbf{Differential privacy as optional enhancement:} The cryptographic guarantee of CKKS protects individual teacher outputs during transit and aggregation. For additional protection of the revealed aggregate, clients can add calibrated Gaussian noise to their features before encryption, achieving $(\epsilon, \delta)$-differential privacy. We note that our prior work demonstrated compatibility with DP at $\epsilon = 9.69$ ($\sigma = 1.0$), and that DP can be layered on top without protocol changes.

\textbf{Residual risk:} The trained student model, like any machine learning model, may encode information about its training data through model inversion or membership inference attacks. This risk is shared with all distillation and federated learning methods and is not specific to our protocol.


=== 21.3.2026 ===

\section{METHODOLOGY}
\label{sec:methodology}

We present a federated knowledge distillation framework in which client teacher outputs remain encrypted throughout server-side processing. The method proceeds in three phases: (1)~client-side local training and feature extraction, (2)~encrypted upload of per-block input--output pairs, and (3)~server-side block-level encrypted distillation into an HE-compatible student. The server operates as a blind compute delegate---it never observes any data, model weights, or intermediate values in plaintext.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/SysFigureSimple.pdf}
\caption{Overview of the HEIFD framework. First, clients independently train local teacher models on their private datasets. Next, clients extract per-block input--output feature pairs, normalise them, and upload them securely to the server encrypted under the CKKS homomorphic encryption scheme. Finally, the central server acts as a completely blind compute delegate, distilling these encrypted supervision signals block-by-block into an HE-compatible student model (e.g., PolyResNet-18) without ever exposing plaintext data, weights, or intermediate activations.}
\label{fig:sys_figure}
\end{figure*}
 
